## Summary

The `message()` tagged template in `@optique/core/message` flattens interpolated `Message` arrays and `MessageTerm` objects into the returned array by reference. This means mutating the returned message can retroactively corrupt the original interpolated values:

```typescript
import { message, optionName } from "@optique/core/message";

const term = optionName("--x");
const inner = message`inner`;

const fromTerm = message`prefix ${term}`;
const fromMessage = message`x ${inner}`;

(fromTerm[1] as any).optionName = "--y";
(fromMessage[1] as any).text = "changed";

console.log(term);       // { type: "optionName", optionName: "--y" } — corrupted!
console.log(inner);      // [{ type: "text", text: "changed" }] — corrupted!
```

The fix clones each interpolated term via the existing `cloneMessageTerm()` helper before pushing it into the result array. This is consistent with how *parser.ts* and *doc.ts* already clone messages at composition boundaries to prevent reference aliasing.

Closes https://github.com/dahlia/optique/issues/505

## Test plan

- Two new regression tests verify that interpolated `MessageTerm` objects and `Message` arrays are structurally equal (`deepEqual`) but referentially distinct (`notEqual`) from the originals.
- All existing tests in *message.test.ts* continue to pass.
- Full `mise test` passes across Deno, Node.js, and Bun.

